WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.020
In Mozambique's Gorongosa National Park, poachers hunted elephants during a civil

2
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.680
war. The war lasted from 1977 to 1992.

3
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.200
Before the war, about one in five sampled females had no tusks.

4
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.060
Among the females that survived, about half had none.

5
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.920
No elephant lost her tusks.

6
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.220
So how did the population change?

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.220
The answer starts with a fact.

8
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.260
Individuals differ.

9
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.280
Variation means the differences among individuals in a population.

10
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.800
An allele is one version of a gene.

11
00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.860
A mutation is a change in DNA.

12
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.100
It is the original source of new alleles.

13
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.360
Gene flow is the movement of alleles from one population to another.

14
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:59.320
Sexual reproduction shuffles alleles that already exist into new combinations.

15
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:04.883
Populations tend to make more young than the environment can support.

16
00:01:05.703 --> 00:01:12.543
A female blue crab in the Chesapeake Bay may carry an egg mass of about 750,000 to

17
00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:17.123
8 million eggs. Only a small fraction become adult crabs.

18
00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:22.303
Most die young. An elephant usually has one calf at a time.

19
00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:25.057
Now add a pressure.

20
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:30.837
A selective pressure is anything in the environment that makes some variants more

21
00:01:30.837 --> 00:01:32.557
likely to survive than others.

22
00:01:33.437 --> 00:01:36.137
In Gorongosa, poachers wanted ivory.

23
00:01:36.637 --> 00:01:39.197
So tusked elephants were killed more often.

24
00:01:40.197 --> 00:01:44.197
Tuskless females already existed, and they survived more often.

25
00:01:44.977 --> 00:01:46.817
That is natural selection.

26
00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:53.777
Individuals with certain inherited variations survive and reproduce more than others.

28
00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:55.647
Now count the survivors.

29
00:01:56.547 --> 00:01:59.527
The share of females with no tusks went up.

30
00:02:00.387 --> 00:02:03.287
Tuskless survivors left more of the next generation.

31
00:02:04.187 --> 00:02:08.647
So that inherited variation becomes more common in later generations.

32
00:02:09.687 --> 00:02:11.547
No single elephant changed.

33
00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:13.427
The population changed.

34
00:02:14.747 --> 00:02:15.747
Individuals do not evolve.

35
00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:17.427
Populations do.

36
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.340
Back to the question.

37
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.960
Why do some individuals survive and reproduce while others do not?

38
00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.120
Because they differ, and some variations suit the conditions better.

39
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.980
Those individuals leave more young.

40
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.660
So those variations become more common.

41
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.460
A strong pressure, like heavy poaching, can change a trait within a few decades.

42
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.860
A weak pressure changes it slowly.

43
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.980
Remember this rule.

44
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.560
Individuals differ, some survive more, and the population changes.
